WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.890
Facebook is banned in China.

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.860
Google is also banned.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.000
But AirBnB isn’t.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.330
I wanted to know why China allows AirBnB to
operate when it’s banned so many large American

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:15.090
tech companies, so I dug into the history
of AirBnB and their operations in China to

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:19.620
figure out what it takes to grow a truly global
tech company in the 21st century.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.460
What I found really surprised me.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:27.260
AirBnB is a great lens to view US-China relations
and in this video I’m going to show you

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:32.070
the China loophole, how it relates to AirBnB’s
history, and what it means for the future

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.480
of American technology companies in China.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.640
The Chinese economy is expected to be the
biggest in the world in a few years after

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.680
all, so this is important stuff.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.430
If you’re traveling to China, you’d need
a VPN just to watch this video.

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:48.800
That’s because YouTube is blocked by the
Great Firewall, which prevents anyone in China

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.421
from accessing basically any website or app
that the government doesn’t have control

00:00:53.421 --> 00:00:54.421
over.

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:58.880
Instagram, Pinterest, and Snapchat are all
blocked, but AirBnB isn’t.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.810
Which struck me as odd, since AirBnB is a
very typical American tech company.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:07.280
They were founded in the Bay Area, are listed
on the NASDAQ, and all three founders are

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:08.280
American citizens.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.200
But AirBnB has employed a few key strategies
to allow them to succeed in China where so

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.820
many tech startups have failed before, and
understanding the choices AirBnB has made

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.120
shines a light on how the Chinese Communist
Party views relations with American companies

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.130
as a whole.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:23.510
Let’s figure this out together.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:31.180
If you’re new here, I’m John Coogan, a
startup founder based in Los Angeles, California,

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:35.020
and on this channel I analyze the strategies
of successful technology entrepreneurs.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.130
I put out new videos weekly, so please hit
that subscribe button and ring the bell to

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:40.130
make sure you don’t miss out.

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.900
China has an entirely unique set of rules
and regulations when it comes to internet

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:44.900
businesses.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:48.630
Taking a business model that’s working in
the United States and expanding internationally

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:52.729
is often alluring because of how many new
customers you could gain.

00:01:52.729 --> 00:01:57.659
If you have product-market fit domestically,
launching that same product in a foreign country

00:01:57.659 --> 00:02:00.200
seems like an easy way to grow your business.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.119
But China is unlike any other country in the
world.

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:08.239
See, China is a one-party state led by the
Chinese Communist Party, and the government

00:02:08.239 --> 00:02:11.450
enforces strict rules around what can be done
on the internet.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:15.581
In America, you can spin up a new startup
with little more than a landing page, but

00:02:15.581 --> 00:02:18.940
expanding your venture into China is a lot
more complicated.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:23.770
AirBnB has taken three key steps to maintain
compliance with the Chinese government and

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:27.900
each one showcases the benefits and costs
of expanding into China.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:32.930
The first hurdle in any American company’s
way is the Internet Content Provider or “ICP”

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:33.930
licence.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:38.990
“The ICP is kind of the golden key, that
opens the gate and allows you inside.”

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:43.250
That’s AirBnB’s former Senior Legal Counsel
stressing the importance of this license.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:47.510
Without it, you literally can’t put up a
single website in China, and it’s far from

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:48.599
a simple process.

00:02:48.599 --> 00:02:53.190
You need a local law firm in China to help
you with the filings and have your web hosting

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.300
provider vetted by Chinese authorities.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:03:01.860
“This is not just any business license you
can do within 21 days, this takes all teams”

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:06.000
AirBnB is a big company though, so they clearly
have the resources to set up a team in China

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.750
to handle this, but the specific structure
they used to build their Chinese division

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:13.400
is unlike any normal corporate structure.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.050
And here’s where it gets really weird.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:19.660
Under Chinese law, foreign ownership in most
Chinese industries is prohibited.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:24.330
Companies like Alibaba aren't allowed to have
non-Chinese shareholders, but there’s a

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:25.330
work around.

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:30.580
It’s called the Variable Interest Entity
or VIE, and this is really the heart of the

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:31.580
China loophole.

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.629
A Variable Interest Entity is essentially
just a shell company.

00:03:34.629 --> 00:03:39.720
The entity is set up to hold all the assets
the company owns in China, but because of

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:44.110
Chinese law, the entity isn’t technically
owned by the controlling company.

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:49.569
So AirBnB created one of these variable interest
entities to operate in China, but it doesn’t

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:50.750
own a single share of it.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:56.220
Instead, there is a complex web of legal agreements
between AirBnB and their Chinese counterpart

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:01.900
that gives AirBnB a claim on the profits belonging
to the shell entity, but there is no recognition

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:03.720
of any actual ownership.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:08.319
This model was actually first developed by
Enron to perpetuate a massive fraud at the

00:04:08.319 --> 00:04:11.319
turn of the millennium and it has major risks.

00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:15.440
If you’ve seen the recent volatility of
Chinese stocks on American exchanges, it’s

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.819
all because of the VIE structure.

00:04:17.819 --> 00:04:22.580
Because AirBnB lacks clear control over their
China operations, there’s a chance that

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:26.439
laws could change and they could have to forfeit
everything very quickly.

00:04:26.439 --> 00:04:30.050
But this is essentially the cost of doing
business in China.

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:34.780
When you can’t own shares in a Chinese company,
it’s basically impossible to set up a proper

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:37.409
subsidiary, like you would in Canada or Mexico.

00:04:37.409 --> 00:04:42.339
These laws make rapid expansion into China
extremely risky, and as we’ve seen, most

00:04:42.339 --> 00:04:45.270
American tech companies fail to gain traction
in China.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:50.059
AirBnB was willing to undertake the challenge
of getting an ICP license and setting up a

00:04:50.059 --> 00:04:54.210
Variable Interest Entity, but that doesn’t
explain why China let them advance through

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:55.740
these stages undeterred.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:00.050
The reason is that China cares a lot more
about censoring information on social media

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:05.120
than restricting commerce, and AirBnB isn’t
really used for spreading information like

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:06.710
Facebook and Twitter are.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:09.259
One of AirBnB’s investors put it really
well:

00:05:09.259 --> 00:05:13.569
“Media and commerce are two separate categories,
and as you know media is a lot more regulated

00:05:13.569 --> 00:05:17.789
and there’s a lot more oversight in that
part of the internet value chain.

00:05:17.789 --> 00:05:20.969
Whereas cross border commerce is being promoted
by Chinese government.”

00:05:20.969 --> 00:05:24.159
Essentially, if you’re going to do business
in China, you’re going to have to hand over

00:05:24.159 --> 00:05:28.449
your data and censor information that the
Chinese Communist Party doesn’t want spreading.

00:05:28.449 --> 00:05:32.399
This requirement has proven to be insurmountable
by many American tech companies.

00:05:32.399 --> 00:05:37.199
Google famously pulled out of China entirely
after refusing to censor search results.

00:05:37.199 --> 00:05:42.430
But AirBnB’s business isn’t built on data
and censorship isn’t as much of an issue,

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:46.289
so these Chinese rules are much less harmful
to their core business.

00:05:46.289 --> 00:05:51.370
In order to understand how AirBnB operates
and the real reason why they actually stand

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:58.620
a chance at launching a successful China operation,
we have to go back to the company’s founding.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:05.659
In 2015, Brian Chesky, the founder of AirBnB
stood on stage with Xi Jinping at a US-China

00:06:05.659 --> 00:06:06.659
technology summit.

00:06:06.659 --> 00:06:11.780
But just 8 years earlier, he was a broke entrepreneur
looking for a way to pay his rent.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:16.810
It was 2007, and Brian was living with his
future AirBnB co-founders although he didn’t

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:17.879
know that at the time.

00:06:17.879 --> 00:06:22.490
He had graduated from the Rhode Island Institute
of Design and recently decided to move to

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:25.020
San Francisco to pursue the dream of building
a startup.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:29.800
“This is October 2007, I get to San Francisco
and Joe tells me the rent is $1,150.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:32.069
I didn’t have enough money for rent.

00:06:32.069 --> 00:06:35.219
That weekend, this international design conference
was coming to San Francisco.

00:06:35.219 --> 00:06:38.069
We went to this conference website, and we
noticed on this conference website, they had

00:06:38.069 --> 00:06:39.069
a hotels tab.

00:06:39.069 --> 00:06:40.069
And we clicked on the hotels tab.

00:06:40.069 --> 00:06:42.039
And in the hotels tab, there are always hotels
and next to every hotel, it said, “sold

00:06:42.039 --> 00:06:43.249
out”, “sold out”, “sold out”.

00:06:43.249 --> 00:06:45.199
And at that point, we just had this idea.

00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:48.869
We said, “well, designers need a place to
stay, we literally have no money, in fact

00:06:48.869 --> 00:06:49.979
I don’t know how I’m gonna make rent.”

00:06:49.979 --> 00:06:54.580
So we thought, what if we just created a bed
and breakfast for the design conference?"

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:57.560
So they created the first AirBnB ever in their
own apartment.

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:02.279
The idea didn’t take off immediately, but
after testing the idea again at a few other

00:07:02.279 --> 00:07:05.529
conferences, they began to think the idea
had traction.

00:07:05.529 --> 00:07:10.919
Beyond saving money on hotels and being able
to find a place to stay on short notice, AirBnB

00:07:10.919 --> 00:07:14.800
created really strong relationships between
hosts and guests.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:18.039
When most people travel, they want to get
to know the city they are staying in and have

00:07:18.039 --> 00:07:19.449
an authentic experience.

00:07:19.449 --> 00:07:24.669
By staying in an AirBnB, a tourist would have
a local contact and get recommendations about

00:07:24.669 --> 00:07:25.949
what to do in town.

00:07:25.949 --> 00:07:30.559
And it’s this idea of building a community
and sharing culture that really drives AirBnB

00:07:30.559 --> 00:07:31.809
to expand globally.

00:07:31.809 --> 00:07:36.121
Airbnb’s mission is to “create a world
where anyone can belong anywhere” and in

00:07:36.121 --> 00:07:41.229
order to achieve that mission, AirBnB has
to be available in every part of the world,

00:07:41.229 --> 00:07:44.339
even if that expansion comes at a very significant
cost.

00:07:44.339 --> 00:07:48.960
But it’s also in AirBnB’s financial interest
to expand globally, even more so than other

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:49.960
tech companies.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:54.279
This is because the short-term vacation rental
market has a global network effect.

00:07:54.279 --> 00:07:58.990
It’s a winner take all market because people
who use AirBnB while traveling from New York

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:03.250
to San Francisco will also want to use the
service while traveling abroad.

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:06.900
And this became a really critical strategy
for the company early on in their journey.

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:10.830
The first few funding rounds for AirBnB were
pretty small by Silicon Valley standards.

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:16.499
They joined Y-Combinator in 2009 and received
$20 thousand dollars in exchange for 6% of

00:08:16.499 --> 00:08:17.499
the company.

00:08:17.499 --> 00:08:21.360
The entire global financial system had just
collapsed and America was still recovering

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.430
from the great recession, so startup valuations
were definitely depressed.

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:29.539
As Brian went out to raise money for AirBnB
after demo day, he wasn’t getting a lot

00:08:29.539 --> 00:08:30.539
of attention.

00:08:30.539 --> 00:08:34.810
It was still difficult for venture capitalists
to wrap their minds around the idea of staying

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:37.139
on an airbed in a strangers house.

00:08:37.139 --> 00:08:40.310
But one venture capitalist knew there was
something special here.

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:45.589
Greg McAdoo at Sequoia Capital saw something
unique in Brian and his team: they had grit.

00:08:45.589 --> 00:08:49.990
One of the biggest concepts Paul Graham, and
really the entire Y-Combinator team tries

00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:53.829
to instill in the startups they fund is the
idea of “staying alive.”

00:08:53.829 --> 00:08:58.660
Far too many entrepreneurs raise money and
then spend it all over a few months, expecting

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:00.000
that they will be able to raise more.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.829
It’s critical to instead focus on keeping
your startup alive for as long as possible,

00:09:04.829 --> 00:09:06.120
by any means necessary.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:10.379
The AirBnB team had scraped by for years doing
whatever it took to make ends meet.

00:09:10.379 --> 00:09:12.420
They just refused to call it quits.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:17.459
During one particularly rough stretch, they
decided to capitalize on all the hype around

00:09:17.459 --> 00:09:20.080
the 2008 election between Barack Obama and
John McCain.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:24.350
Leveraging their design backgrounds, the team
put together custom cereal boxes with each

00:09:24.350 --> 00:09:28.560
candidate's name on them and sold them as
collectors items at the conventions.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.509
Even though this wasn’t a scalable business
and really had nothing to do with AirBnB directly,

00:09:32.509 --> 00:09:37.080
they made $25 thousand dollars and were able
to keep the company alive for a few months

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:38.080
longer.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:44.240
This story resonated with Paul Graham, who
accepted AirBnB into the 2009 YC cohort, and

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:49.610
when Greg McAdoo asked PG what team had the
most grit, it was an easy answer.

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:54.529
That willingness to grind it out when times
were tough allowed AirBnB to survive long

00:09:54.529 --> 00:10:00.079
enough to raise more money, and I think this
determination is a big reason why AirBnB has

00:10:00.079 --> 00:10:02.900
been able to overcome so many challenges in
China.

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:07.069
Brian and the AirBnB team were able to raise
a $600 thousand dollar seed round after demo

00:10:07.069 --> 00:10:11.340
day and quickly followed that with a $7 million
dollar series A in 2010.

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:15.420
These are pretty typical figures for young
startups, and are even a little low by present

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:19.899
day standards, but AirBnB’s Series B round
was where things really changed.

00:10:19.899 --> 00:10:24.519
They raised a massive $112 million dollars
from Andreessen Horowitz, and the reason was

00:10:24.519 --> 00:10:26.660
clear: AirBnB had to go global.

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:31.630
A group called Rocket Internet had launched
an AirBnB clone in Europe called Wimdu and

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:35.060
this was a major threat to AirBnB’s long
term plans.

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:40.189
Within 3 months of closing the Series B, AirBnB
had opened its first international office

00:10:40.189 --> 00:10:41.189
in London.

00:10:41.189 --> 00:10:46.180
AirBnB wound up beating Wimdu and establishing
a strong foothold in Europe, but Asia would

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:47.779
be another matter entirely.

00:10:47.779 --> 00:10:52.220
AirBnB wanted to create a flywheel of guests
becoming hosts and vice versa.

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:56.329
People would start by using AirBnB while traveling
for work, like what happened at that first

00:10:56.329 --> 00:11:00.050
design conference, but then they would start
using it for vacations.

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:04.250
The more they traveled using AirBnB, the more
likely they were to list their own home on

00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:06.610
AirBnB when they had free space.

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:10.209
Leaving international markets up for grabs
was extremely risky.

00:11:10.209 --> 00:11:14.889
If American travelers wound up using a different
app while traveling abroad, they could easily

00:11:14.889 --> 00:11:18.600
wind up using that app while traveling domestically
as well.

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:23.610
If one country stops using AirBnB, there could
be a domino effect in other countries.

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:28.410
China is the final frontier in AirBnB’s
plan to become a truly global company and

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:33.949
in order to win, AirBnB has to be a great
experience for both Chinese hosts, and Chinese

00:11:33.949 --> 00:11:35.139
travelers abroad.

00:11:35.139 --> 00:11:38.120
Here’s AirBnB’s co-founder explaining
their full rationale:

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:41.550
“So, Chinese travelers are the biggest spender
on international tourism.

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:42.550
It’s at this point double or triple that
of the United States or Germany which is number

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:43.550
2 or 3 respectively.

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:44.600
So it’s a huge outbound market.

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:46.143
So that alone is a reason to prioritize it.”

00:11:46.143 --> 00:11:47.143
That’s Nate Blecharczyk.

00:11:47.143 --> 00:11:50.480
He’s a co-founder of AirBnB and the Chairman
of Airbnb China, and he’s a really critical

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:51.680
part of this story.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:57.470
When he says “outbound market” what he
means is Chinese tourists staying at Airbnb’s

00:11:57.470 --> 00:11:58.470
while abroad.

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:01.260
But there’s a second critical piece to AirBnB’s
China strategy:

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:02.260
“The second part though is, you know, this
is a marketplace networks effects business

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:06.200
and so actually more than 50% of our travelers
going to destinations in Asia are coming from

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:07.200
China.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:13.420
And so by attracting Chinese guests from China,
we’re actually helping to grow our business

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:16.230
in Japan, in Thailand, throughout Asia.“

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:20.279
Other tech companies can afford to cut ties
with China if they don’t have as strong

00:12:20.279 --> 00:12:21.790
of a global network effect.

00:12:21.790 --> 00:12:27.399
But losing China may cause AirBnB hosts in
Japan or Thailand to switch to a competing

00:12:27.399 --> 00:12:30.129
service, which could cause a domino effect.

00:12:30.129 --> 00:12:34.779
When a user decides to list their home on
AirBnB, they want to be sure that any traveler,

00:12:34.779 --> 00:12:39.499
no matter what country they're coming from,
can book a reservation without any issues.

00:12:39.499 --> 00:12:42.149
And that’s why China is so critical.

00:12:42.149 --> 00:12:46.000
AirBnB clearly needs to maintain operations
in China.

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:50.720
Their entire Asia business depends on it,
but sharing customer data with foreign governments

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:55.110
is a tricky issue that is starting to play
a bigger role in the national dialogue.

00:12:55.110 --> 00:12:59.680
Chinese apps like TikTok have come under fire
for collecting data on US consumers while

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:01.980
ultimately answering to the Chinese government.

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:06.589
As AirBnB has grown their operations in China,
they have had to make compromises to the way

00:13:06.589 --> 00:13:08.120
they handle customer data.

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:13.449
Since 2016, Airbnb has told users that it
shares information with Chinese authorities.

00:13:13.449 --> 00:13:18.370
Phone numbers, email addresses, and in-app
messages are all made available to the government

00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:22.110
when you check into a Chinese Airbnb, but
this doesn’t really surprise me.

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:26.139
I was in China a few years ago and just assumed
that the government could see anything I was

00:13:26.139 --> 00:13:31.089
doing on my phone, and I expect most Airbnb
guests traveling in China would assume the

00:13:31.089 --> 00:13:32.089
same.

00:13:32.089 --> 00:13:35.360
While I would never want to live in a place
that restricted personal freedom and privacy

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:39.839
to such an extent, I don’t think there’s
much that a company like Airbnb can do about

00:13:39.839 --> 00:13:40.839
it.

00:13:40.839 --> 00:13:44.100
Nate Blecharczyk actually made headlines last
year when the Wall Street Journal reported

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:50.200
that he had said “We’re not here to promote
American values” - that can seem callus,

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:54.720
like he’s someone that doesn’t see privacy
as a universal right, but I think he’s just

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:56.519
doing what’s best for Airbnb.

00:13:56.519 --> 00:14:01.029
Ultimately, pulling out of China would mean
sacrificing not just their Chinese business,

00:14:01.029 --> 00:14:04.860
but much of their Asia business more broadly,
and it wouldn’t do anything to change how

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:07.190
privacy is handled in China.

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:11.580
Local competitors would just step up to fill
the void while gladly giving full access to

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:12.580
their data.

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:16.930
If you’d like to go a layer deeper, just
enter your email on JohnCoogan.com, and please

00:14:16.930 --> 00:14:20.890
check out this recommended video, the YouTube
algorithm thinks you’ll really like it.

